short lines against full lines (what each short line leaves out):
  SHORT: The machine. It is Fortress3, which Ashlock and Ashlock (2006) found in evolving populations of machines of this sort.
       replace | full: sort (automata). Its rules, as the Axelrod library of strategies gives them, match mine exactly. Their abstract (the summary at the paper's head, and all I have read of it) mentions no slips, so their populations may have reached the machine without any. | short: sort.
  SHORT: Why it holds its ground. Iwasaki, Joe, Kandori, Obara and Yokoo (2012), who have the same three states, begun at the cooperating one, under the name two-period mutual punishment.
       replace | full: (2012) | short: (2012), who
       delete | full: They went through every machine with up to three states, asked of each whether any possible way of playing could gain against it when each player can misread the other's move, and singled this one out, for misreadings that each player makes on its own. Where both players misread together, they found that it fails and tit-for-tat (cooperate first, then do whatever the other player did last) does best. | short: 
  SHORT: That mistakes are what make such a way of playing stable. Boyd (1989); and that without mistakes none is, Boyd and Lorberbaum (1987).
       replace | full: (1987), which I know only by its title and by what others say of it. | short: (1987).
  SHORT: That a few mistakes can help cooperation. Miller (1996); Zhang (2018).
       replace | full: (1996) wrote so, though the average payoff in his runs fell; and | short: (1996);
       replace | full: (2018), in whose abstract the machine that succeeds when there are errors is win-stay, lose-shift, which is the one-defection machine of "Why two defections and not one" begun at its other state. | short: (2018).
  SHORT: That without slips cooperation comes and goes. Van Veelen and others (2012).
      (the same as the full line)
  SHORT: That a machine which lasts must sometimes defect first. Binmore and Samuelson (1992); my argument above is theirs. Knight and others (2017) had already called Fortress3's opening a handshake.
      (the same as the full line)

